WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:04.370
-My first guest tonight is
a 16-time Grammy winner.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.105
Grammy winner!

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:09.508
And he is in the Rock &amp; Roll
Hall of Fame. Well deserved.

00:00:09.508 --> 00:00:13.279
His band Foo Fighters are
celebrating more than 25 years

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.381
of making awesome music
together with the release

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:17.984
of their new album,
"Medicine at Midnight."

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:19.352
Listen to it now.

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.820
Here is Dave Grohl!

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.124
Oh, my goodness, Dave Grohl,
it is great to see you.

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:27.660
Thanks so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.297
Congrats on the new album,
"Medicine at Midnight."

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:35.435
This just came out on Friday.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:39.605
But you were going to release it
last year, correct?

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:42.942
-Well, yeah. So, we actually --

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:47.513
We started recording this record
like mid-2019.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:48.982
And we were like,

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:53.752
"2020 is going to be the best
year our band has ever had."

00:00:53.752 --> 00:00:56.188
We're like,
"It's our 25th anniversary.

00:00:56.188 --> 00:00:59.191
It's our 10th record.
We're going to tour the world.

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:02.395
There's, like, stadiums.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:04.230
So let's make a party record.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.400
Yeah, we'll make these songs
that have, like, groove,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.968
and they're going to make people
bounce.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.737
And it's going to be
the stadiums --

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.372
festivals are gonna --" And then
everyone went just pbht!

00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:15.975
-[ Laughs ]
-No way it's gonna happen.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.745
And so we basically
just sat on it.

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.880
We were like, "Okay, let's wait.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.816
Let's wait for a while and see
if things go back to 'normal.'"

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:25.518
And then after
like eight months,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:28.254
it was like,
"Dude, just put it out!

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:30.422
Come on!
Let's give it to the people.

00:01:30.422 --> 00:01:32.158
Let's go."
So that's it.

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:34.760
-How does it feel to
finally have it out?

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.097
-I mean, I've never given birth
before.

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:40.866
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:40.866 --> 00:01:44.237
-Women hate when you compare
anything to childbirth.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:47.140
-Uh-huh.
-But honestly, it's just like --

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.774
[ Groaning ]
And they're like --

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:50.743
[ Mimicking crying ]

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:56.082
And then you just give it up
for adoption, is what you do.

00:01:56.082 --> 00:02:01.453
-I love it 'cause there's so
many different sounds on this.

00:02:01.453 --> 00:02:04.657
Someone has called it
your party record.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.427
What inspired the different
sounds in this one?

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:09.328
Just the 25 years?

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:10.729
-Yeah, I mean, honestly, like,

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.333
we looked back at all of
the things that we'd done.

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:16.035
And, you know, we've done,
like, loud, dissonant,

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:19.071
heavy, sort of punk rock,
crazy music.

00:02:19.071 --> 00:02:21.207
And then, we've also done,
like, lots of acoustic stuff,

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:24.743
and stuff that's more
orchestrated and gentle,

00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:26.145
and then everything in between.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:31.384
But the one thing that we had
never really done was make

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:33.987
kind of, like, you know,
like a groove-oriented --

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:35.554
it's hard to call it
a dance record.

00:02:35.554 --> 00:02:38.257
Listen, if I say --
-No.

00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:40.727
-If you put the word "dance"
in the same sentence

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:42.128
as Foo Fighters,

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:44.263
people are like,
"I think I might throw --

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:45.564
I think I just threw up
in my mouth."

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:46.866
Like, "You can't do that."

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.200
-I wouldn't say
it's a dance record. No.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.202
But there are some grooves
on here.

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:53.572
The title track is my jam,
"Medicine at Midnight."

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:57.243
It's kind of like a Bowie,

00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:00.046
like almost like
a "Let's Dance-y" type of --

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:02.581
there might even be like --
[ Mimicking wood tapping ]

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.183
there's that woodblock
or something happening.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:06.352
You're like, "Mm! Oh!"

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.488
-It's got it in there.
Well, this is the thing.

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.956
It's like, okay, so,
we're roughly the same age,

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:13.459
grew up listening to rock bands
that made albums

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:15.928
that you could do
the Molly Ringwald dance to,

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:17.796
and, like, you know,
albums that you could,

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:20.065
like, kind of get down to,
like David Bowie records

00:03:20.065 --> 00:03:22.868
or Rolling Stones records,
and even long before that,

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:24.537
Sly and the Family Stone
kind of stuff.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.572
We were all raised on
that type of music.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:28.574
We never really figured that --

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:31.244
We never thought we would apply
that to what we do.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.845
But I was like, "Why not?"
-Yeah.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.947
-You're like, "At this point,
it's like everything

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:38.751
I say is a dad joke,
everything I wear is a dad --

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.353
they're, like, sweatpants
and Crocs or whatever."

00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:44.023
So, I'm like, "Okay, it's time
to -- let's do this.

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:46.859
Like, I want to see a stadium
doing this."

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:47.760
-Why not?
-You know?

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.796
-No, it's such a good groove.

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:52.599
You know I was gonna ask you,
as far as Bowie,

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:55.334
have you ever -- did you ever
work with Bowie or meet him?

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.903
-I did.

00:03:57.903 --> 00:03:59.538
A long, long time ago,

00:03:59.538 --> 00:04:03.709
he had his 50th birthday party
at Madison Square Garden.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.179
I think it was like 1997 maybe.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:10.649
And we were invited to play
at his birthday party.

00:04:10.649 --> 00:04:11.951
There were a lot of
other artists there.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:14.153
Billy Corgan from
The Smashing Pumpkins was there,

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:18.157
Robert Smith from The Cure was
there, Charles from the Pixies,

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:20.259
all these different artists,
Sonic Youth.

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:22.461
And so we got to meet there.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:27.266
And then not long after that,
he was in Los Angeles,

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:31.738
and I was asked by his guitarist
if I wanted to come play drums

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:33.539
on a song that he was recording.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.107
So I got to record with Bowie.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:37.542
I got to watch him
step in front of a microphone,

00:04:37.542 --> 00:04:40.279
do one take perfectly,

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:44.817
and the sound out of his mouth
was David Bowie.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.218
It absolutely blew my mind.

00:04:46.218 --> 00:04:49.221
And then years later,

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:52.791
I was asked to do a song
for a movie soundtrack.

00:04:52.791 --> 00:04:55.194
They didn't want the Foo
Fighters. They just wanted me.

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:57.597
But I didn't feel comfortable
just doing a solo thing.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.298
So I thought, "Oh, I'll do
an instrumental track

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:00.532
and have someone else sing."

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.868
So I contacted Bowie.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.837
And I said,
"Here, listen to this thing.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:06.605
Maybe you'd like to sing on it."

00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:09.409
And time went by.
I didn't hear anything.

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:13.446
Finally, I got an e-mail back,
and he said,

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:18.651
"David, I like the instrumental,
but this time --"

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.354
It was, like, a Marvel movie
kind of thing.

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.622
He's like,
"This is not really my trip,

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:26.592
not my genre of film and stuff.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.394
I'd love to work with you
at some point.

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:30.696
But I don't think
this is the time."

00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:32.831
And so I'm sitting in my living
room reading this like,

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:34.734
"Oh, my God,
David Bowie writes e-mails.

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:36.135
This is blowing my mind
right now."

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:39.305
So I immediately e-mailed back
something very --

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:40.706
I didn't want to take up
more of his time.

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:44.777
-Yep.
-So I said,

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:48.914
"Thanks for responding.
I hope you're well. Take care."

00:05:48.914 --> 00:05:53.286
And then one minute later
he e-mails back and he says,

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:55.254
"Well, that's settled then.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.856
Now [bleep] off."

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:59.158
-[ Laughs ] Come on. Come on!

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:02.161
-And I'm like, "Oh, my God.
Holy crap.

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.162
David Bowie just told me
to [bleep] off."

00:06:04.162 --> 00:06:08.201
And so I e-mailed back,
and I hadn't seen him

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:09.935
since his birthday party
at Madison Square Garden.

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:12.504
I said, "Well, I guess I'll see
you in another 15 years."

00:06:12.504 --> 00:06:15.141
And he goes,
"Not if I see you first."

00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:16.308
-He's being funny.

00:06:16.308 --> 00:06:18.777
-And I'm like --
Well, I didn't know that!

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:22.614
I was like, "David Bowie
thinks I'm an [bleep]!"

00:06:22.614 --> 00:06:25.017
And so we went back and forth
until I realized like,

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:26.752
"Oh, he's just being funny."
Whatever.

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:28.220
But, yes, he was
an incredible guy.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.522
And we wound up having
these really funny

00:06:30.522 --> 00:06:32.558
e-mail exchanges
just out of the blue.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:37.263
Like, he would e-mail me
in like June and say,

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:39.231
"Well, happy new year."

00:06:39.231 --> 00:06:41.667
And I'd be like, "What?"

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:46.605
And so then we'd go
back and forth, and I'd say,

00:06:46.605 --> 00:06:48.140
"Drink some day, yes?"

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.711
And he'd immediately e-mail back
and say, "I will, thanks."

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:55.080
And I'm like --
I couldn't tell, you know.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.016
I'd say, oh, like,
he's on a health kick.

00:06:57.016 --> 00:07:00.085
I'm like, "Nuts and muffins?"

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:02.922
And he'd e-mail back and say,
"Those are my attorneys."

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:05.224
-[ Laughs ]
-[Bleep] like that.

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:08.327
-Nuts and muffins.

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.296
He's brilliantly funny.

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:12.565
I remember I went to
some event once.

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:15.134
And I went,
and I honestly put on a --

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:18.604
I was such a fashion victim,
but I put on a long raincoat,

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:21.039
like, a tan raincoat
or something.

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:23.442
And I walked in,
and I see Bowie.

00:07:23.442 --> 00:07:24.843
It's full-on
"Dancing in the Street."

00:07:24.843 --> 00:07:27.580
Basically I'm wearing that,
but I can't pull it off

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:29.449
because I don't have
his face or anything.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.917
So I walk in,
and he's there with Iman,

00:07:31.917 --> 00:07:34.053
and I go, "How are you?

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:37.323
He goes, "Raincoat? Really?"

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:39.692
I was like,
"It's the whole thing."

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:40.826
It's so embarrassing.
-Jimmy, oh, my God!

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:42.895
You know what? Listen.
I just remembered.

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:45.130
Remember the Fourth of July
at the White House?

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:46.832
-[ Laughing ] Oh, my God.
-Remember the Fourth of July

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:51.036
at the White House?
I just remembered this.

00:07:51.036 --> 00:07:52.905
-I was sweating my brains out.

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:55.608
-I know because you were wearing
a three-piece suit.

00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:57.910
You looked like Mark Twain.
I don't know.

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.578
You showed up
to the White House,

00:07:59.578 --> 00:08:03.081
and I remember we walked
upstairs to meet the President,

00:08:03.081 --> 00:08:05.918
and he's wearing, like, khakis
and Izod or whatever.

00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:08.020
And remember?
He was like,

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.989
"Damn, man, you're a little
overdressed for a picnic."

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:12.425
It was a picnic!
-Totally making fun of me.

00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:15.094
Michelle -- They were laughing
at me, because you guys come in.

00:08:15.094 --> 00:08:16.495
I see you. I go --

00:08:16.495 --> 00:08:18.397
I'm opening up
for the Foo Fighters there.

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:20.165
It's for veterans.

00:08:20.165 --> 00:08:21.434
It was an outdoor thing.

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:22.868
I'll do a little stand-up,
something.

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:24.336
I'm wearing a three-piece suit.

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:25.838
I've never been invited to
the White House.

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:27.973
I'm freaking out.
I see you. I go, "What's up?"

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:29.608
And you're just cool

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:31.543
and you're wearing, like,
board shorts or whatever.

00:08:31.543 --> 00:08:33.712
And you were like,
"Yeah, dude, are you hot?"

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:37.082
And I go, "Yeah, I am.
It's like 100 degrees."

00:08:37.082 --> 00:08:39.885
And Taylor was like -- Dude.
I think Pat was like --

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:43.623
You all were kind of laughing
like, "Put a T-shirt on, man.

00:08:43.623 --> 00:08:45.657
You don't have to.
It's an outdoor event."

00:08:45.657 --> 00:08:47.025
I go, "I'm fine."

00:08:47.025 --> 00:08:48.494
And we all walked up.

00:08:48.494 --> 00:08:49.895
You guys talked to the President

00:08:49.895 --> 00:08:52.431
and Michelle for a while,
the First Lady.

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:55.367
And then I got walked in,
totally covered in sweat.

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:58.237
Sweating through my collar.
It was a different color shirt.

00:08:58.237 --> 00:08:59.605
And they both were laughing.

00:08:59.605 --> 00:09:02.107
And Michelle was like,
"Can I loosen your tie?"

00:09:02.107 --> 00:09:04.343
And, yeah,
President Barack Obama said,

00:09:04.343 --> 00:09:07.813
"Jimmy, didn't you get the memo?
It's a barbecue."

00:09:07.813 --> 00:09:09.114
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:09.114 --> 00:09:10.816
That's right! I remember.

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:13.285
But you know what?
The other thing I remember is,

00:09:13.285 --> 00:09:15.287
like, when we first got there,
of course, you know,

00:09:15.287 --> 00:09:16.856
there's Secret Service

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:18.457
and all this security and stuff
like that.

00:09:18.457 --> 00:09:20.459
And we're like --
It's us and a road crew.

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:22.795
So, like, we're like pirates,
okay?

00:09:22.795 --> 00:09:23.829
We're setting up.

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:25.330
And there was someone
that briefed us,

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.466
and they basically said,
"Here's the deal.

00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:29.969
If anyone has
to use the bathroom,

00:09:29.969 --> 00:09:32.704
there's bathrooms over there,
there's bathrooms over there.

00:09:32.704 --> 00:09:35.307
Whatever you do,
don't go pee in the bushes,

00:09:35.307 --> 00:09:37.076
because there's people
in the bushes."

00:09:37.076 --> 00:09:39.711
We're like, "Wow!"

00:09:39.711 --> 00:09:41.380
-It's the White House.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:42.715
It's the White House.
-Yeah.

